    "body": "## Description\n\nCurrently all code lives in a single `src/main.cpp` file. As the browser grows, this will become unmaintainable. Split the code into a clear structure with separate header and implementation files, following standard C++ conventions.\n\nThis is a **refactor** — the visible behavior must not change. After this change, the browser should still open a window titled \"HoneyBee Browser\" with the same placeholder text.\n\n## Steps to Complete\n\n1. Create `src/browser/` directory.\n2. Move the `HoneyBeeWindow` class into `src/browser/window.hpp` (declaration) and `src/browser/window.cpp` (implementation).\n3. Move the `HoneyBeeApp` class into `src/browser/app.hpp` and `src/browser/app.cpp`.\n4. Reduce `src/main.cpp` to just the `main()` function that instantiates `HoneyBeeApp` and runs it.\n5. Update `src/CMakeLists.txt` to compile all `.cpp` files in `src/browser/`.\n6. Rebuild and run. The window must behave identically to before.\n7. Commit with `refactor(browser): split window and app classes into separate files`.\n8. Open a pull request.\n\n## Learning Outcomes\n\n- Structuring C++ projects with header/implementation separation.\n- Understanding CMake source file discovery.\n- Learning the difference between declarations (`.hpp`) and definitions (`.cpp`).\n\n## Helpful Resources\n\n- [C++ Core Guidelines: Header files](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#S-philosophy)\n- [CMake: add_executable](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/command/add_executable.html)\n- [gtkmm4 Book: Hello World](https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/gtkmm-documentation/)\n",
